From 88582f9fb6577593678ed1fdc53a0c260d38506d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 4 Jun 2026 17:53:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: patch 0092 -- ring + DMA-buffer silence-flush export
 (V108 render-window CPU-steal stall)

DOS needs this because the engine has no way to silence the SB16 physical
audio path before a long fully-blocking synth render. The organya cold-render
(_org_render_song, ~13.6 s, no cooperative yield) parks the SDL audio thread,
the only ring producer; the producer write cursor freezes. The SB16 auto-init
DMA keeps physically looping the DMA double-buffer and the IRQ-5 ISR keeps
refilling DMA halves from whatever STALE music bytes remain in the software
ring -- so the last music fragment buzzes / tempo-tanks for the whole render
window on slow 486-class HW (Task #7).

The existing exports cannot do this from the engine: SDL/0088 RingFill*() are
read-only cursor queries; SDL_DOSAudioPump() (SDL/0067) is mix-then-commit and
writes whatever the mixer produced -- and if the engine stops the song first so
the mix is silent, the SDL/0054 Shape A silence-detect SKIPS the ring write
entirely, leaving the stale bytes resident. No engine-reachable path memsets
the ring, and none reaches the DMA double-buffer (ISR-only).

SDL_DOSAudioFlushRingSilence() zeroes BOTH physical buffers (software ring +
DMA double-buffer) and collapses the ring cursors to empty under a brief
ISR-atomic cli window, so the silence takes effect immediately even if IRQ-5
refill is delayed during the blocking render (the DX2-class regime DOSBox-X
cannot reproduce). Returns the silence frames flushed (== ring capacity) for
the flush-instr witness silence_frames_flushed >= ring_capacity_frames.

Unconditional from SDL's side (mirrors SDL_DOSAudioPump); the engine gates the
call with a killswitch hint at the nxengine-evo/0206 + 0209 cold-render sites
(correctness fix, default-ON + killswitch). Workspace-local; never upstreamed.
---
 include/SDL3/SDL_dosaudio_pump.h |  94 ++++++++++++++++++++
 src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c  | 142 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 236 insertions(+)

diff --git a/include/SDL3/SDL_dosaudio_pump.h b/include/SDL3/SDL_dosaudio_pump.h
index 586423fcf..44ead6553 100644
--- a/include/SDL3/SDL_dosaudio_pump.h
+++ b/include/SDL3/SDL_dosaudio_pump.h
@@ -310,6 +310,100 @@ extern SDL_DECLSPEC int SDLCALL SDL_DOSAudioRingFillMs(void);
  */
 extern SDL_DECLSPEC int SDLCALL SDL_DOSAudioRingCapacityMs(void);
 
+/**
+ * Hard-silence the SB16 physical audio path: zero the entire software ring
+ * AND the DMA double-buffer, and collapse the ring cursors to empty.
+ *
+ * **DOSKUTSU local addition (patches/SDL/0092, never upstreamed.)**
+ *
+ * Called by the engine immediately BEFORE a long fully-blocking synth render
+ * (the organya cold-render `_org_render_song`, ~13.6 s with no cooperative
+ * yield: nxengine-evo/0206 post-load entry render + nxengine-evo/0209
+ * deferred-cache drain). During that window the SDL audio thread never runs
+ * (`DOSSOUNDBLASTER_PlayDevice` is the only ring producer and it is parked),
+ * so the producer write cursor is frozen; the SB16 auto-init DMA keeps
+ * physically looping the DMA double-buffer, and the IRQ-5 ISR keeps refilling
+ * DMA halves from whatever STALE music bytes remain in the software ring.
+ * On slow 486-class hardware the last music fragment buzzes / tempo-tanks for
+ * the whole render window (the V108 render-window CPU-steal stall, Task #7).
+ *
+ * ## Why the existing exports cannot do this from the engine
+ *
+ * - `SDL_DOSAudioRingFillFrames` / `...Ms` / `...CapacityMs` (SDL/0088) are
+ *   READ-ONLY cursor queries.
+ * - `SDL_DOSAudioPump` (SDL/0067) is a mix-then-commit path: it writes
+ *   whatever the MIXER produced. If the current song is still mixing it writes
+ *   MORE music; if the engine first stops the song so the mix is silent, the
+ *   SDL/0054 Shape A silence-detect SKIPS the ring write entirely (the
+ *   `all_silence` branch in `DOSSOUNDBLASTER_PlayDevice`) -- so the stale song
+ *   bytes already resident in the ring are never overwritten. No engine-
+ *   reachable path memsets the ring, and NONE reaches the DMA double-buffer
+ *   (it is touched only by the IRQ-5 ISR).
+ *
+ * This export zeroes BOTH physical buffers so the silence takes effect
+ * immediately even if IRQ-5 servicing is delayed during the blocking render
+ * (the DX2-class timing regime DOSBox-X cannot reproduce). The DMA double-
+ * buffer is zeroed directly so the auto-init DMA hardware loops silence on its
+ * next sample fetch, not on the next successful ISR refill; the ring is zeroed
+ * and its cursors collapsed so the ISR underruns to its silence-memset path.
+ *
+ * ## Engine-side gate
+ *
+ * Unconditional from SDL's side (mirrors `SDL_DOSAudioPump`): the export always
+ * silences when called. The decision to call lives engine-side, gated by the
+ * engine's killswitch hint at the nxengine-evo/0206 + 0209 call sites
+ * (correctness fix, default-ON + killswitch). No SDL-side env var.
+ *
+ * \returns the total silence frames flushed across BOTH physical buffers
+ *          (software ring + DMA double-buffer) on success -- equal to
+ *          SDL_DOSAudioPhysicalCapacityFrames(). A **nonzero return guarantees
+ *          BOTH buffers were fully zeroed** (self-contained PASS: the caller's
+ *          test is simply `return > 0`, no cross-channel denominator math).
+ *          Returns **0 on any failure/partial path**: no device open, the DMA
+ *          double-buffer (the buffer the SB16 actually loops) not allocated, or
+ *          unknown frame size -- in which case NOTHING is partially zeroed.
+ *
+ * \threadsafety Call from the engine main thread only -- not from the audio
+ *               thread and not from an interrupt context. The cursor collapse
+ *               is performed under a brief DOS_DisableInterrupts window so it
+ *               is ISR-atomic; the bulk buffer memsets run outside that window
+ *               (a concurrent ISR or DMA fetch at worst observes a one-chunk
+ *               silence transition, which is the intended effect).
+ *
+ * \since This function is available since SDL/0092 (doskutsu workspace-local).
+ *
+ * \sa SDL_DOSAudioPhysicalCapacityFrames
+ * \sa SDL_DOSAudioPump
+ */
+extern SDL_DECLSPEC int SDLCALL SDL_DOSAudioFlushRingSilence(void);
+
+/**
+ * True total physical capacity that can hold stale music between the engine
+ * producer and the DAC, in frames -- the denominator for the flush-instr
+ * silence witness (`silence_frames_flushed >= ring_capacity_frames`).
+ *
+ * **DOSKUTSU local addition (patches/SDL/0092, never upstreamed.)**
+ *
+ * This is the software ring (`isr_ring_size`) PLUS the DMA double-buffer (the
+ * buffer the SB16 auto-init DMA physically cycles), converted to frames. It is
+ * deliberately NOT `SDL_DOSAudioRingCapacityMs()` / `SDL_DOSAudioRingFillFrames()`
+ * (SDL/0088): those report the SOFTWARE ring ONLY and miss the DMA double-
+ * buffer, which would let the silence witness FALSE-PASS while the SB16 still
+ * loops the music left in the DMA buffer. Bind the witness denominator to this
+ * value. `SDL_DOSAudioFlushRingSilence()` returns a count equal to this on a
+ * full flush.
+ *
+ * \returns the total physical silence-flush capacity in frames, or 0 when no
+ *          device is open.
+ *
+ * \threadsafety Safe to call from the engine main thread; lock-free.
+ *
+ * \since This function is available since SDL/0092 (doskutsu workspace-local).
+ *
+ * \sa SDL_DOSAudioFlushRingSilence
+ */
+extern SDL_DECLSPEC int SDLCALL SDL_DOSAudioPhysicalCapacityFrames(void);
+
 #ifdef __cplusplus
 }
 #endif
diff --git a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
index ef73f955e..6019cfa2b 100644
--- a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
+++ b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
@@ -962,6 +962,148 @@ int SDL_DOSAudioRingCapacityMs(void)
     return (int)(((Sint64)cap_frames * 1000) / freq);
 }
 
+/* ========================================================================
+ * DOSKUTSU patch (SDL/0092): ring + DMA-buffer silence-flush export
+ * ========================================================================
+ * Engine-callable (main thread) hard-silence of the SB16 physical audio path,
+ * called by the engine immediately BEFORE a long fully-blocking synth render
+ * (the organya cold-render _org_render_song, ~13.6 s with no cooperative
+ * yield: nxengine-evo/0206 post-load entry render + nxengine-evo/0209
+ * deferred-cache drain). During that window the SDL audio thread never runs
+ * (DOSSOUNDBLASTER_PlayDevice is the only ring producer and it is parked), so
+ * isr_ring_write is frozen; the SB16 auto-init DMA keeps physically looping
+ * the DMA double-buffer, and the IRQ-5 ISR keeps refilling DMA halves from
+ * whatever STALE music bytes remain in the software ring. On slow 486-class
+ * HW the last music fragment buzzes / tempo-tanks for the whole render window
+ * (V108 render-window CPU-steal stall; Task #7).
+ *
+ * Why the existing exports cannot do this from the engine:
+ *   - SDL/0088 SDL_DOSAudioRingFill*() are READ-ONLY cursor queries.
+ *   - SDL_DOSAudioPump() (SDL/0067) is a mix-then-commit path: it writes
+ *     whatever the MIXER produced. If the current song is still mixing it
+ *     writes MORE music; if the engine first stops the song so the mix is
+ *     silent, the SDL/0054 Shape A silence-detect SKIPS the ring write
+ *     entirely (the all_silence branch in DOSSOUNDBLASTER_PlayDevice) -- the
+ *     stale song bytes already resident in the ring are never overwritten.
+ *     There is no engine-reachable path that memsets the ring, and NONE that
+ *     reaches the DMA double-buffer (isr_dma_buffer is touched only by the ISR).
+ *
+ * This export zeroes BOTH physical buffers so the silence takes effect
+ * immediately even if IRQ-5 servicing is delayed during the blocking render
+ * (the DX2-class regime DOSBox-X cannot reproduce -- [[dosbox_not_proxy]]):
+ *   1. memset the entire software ring to the device silence value, so any
+ *      subsequent ISR RingCopyOut pulls silence.
+ *   2. memset the entire DMA double-buffer to silence, so the auto-init DMA
+ *      hardware loops silence on its next sample fetch -- not on the next
+ *      successful IRQ refill. (The DMA controller reads independent of CPU
+ *      interrupts, so a cli would not make this atomic vs the hardware anyway;
+ *      a one-sample click at the silence transition is intended.)
+ *   3. collapse the cursors ISR-atomically (isr_ring_read = isr_ring_write,
+ *      mirrored into the hidden producer cursors) so avail == 0 and the ISR
+ *      takes its underrun-silence memset path regardless of ring content.
+ *
+ * Returns the number of silence frames flushed across the software ring
+ * (== ring capacity in frames; the flush-instr witness asserts
+ * silence_frames_flushed >= ring_capacity_frames). 0 when no device is open.
+ *
+ * Unconditional from SDL's side (mirrors SDL_DOSAudioPump): the export always
+ * silences when called; the decision to call lives engine-side, gated by the
+ * engine's killswitch hint at the nxengine-evo/0206 + 0209 call sites
+ * (correctness fix, default-ON + killswitch per [[iter_discipline]]).
+ * Workspace-local; never upstreamed. */
+int SDL_DOSAudioFlushRingSilence(void)
+{
+    SDL_AudioDevice *device = g_dos_audio_device;
+    if (device == NULL || isr_ring_buffer == NULL) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    const int bpf = g_dos_ring_bytes_per_frame;
+    /* Self-contained witness contract (flush-instr): a NONZERO return must
+     * GUARANTEE that BOTH the software ring AND the DMA double-buffer were
+     * fully zeroed. So if the DMA double-buffer (the buffer the SB16 actually
+     * loops -- the load-bearing zero) is not allocated, or the frame size is
+     * unknown, return 0 (failure) BEFORE doing any partial work. The caller's
+     * PASS test is simply "return > 0"; no cross-channel denominator math. */
+    if (isr_dma_buffer == NULL || isr_dma_halfdma <= 0 || bpf <= 0) {
+        doskutsu_dbg_log("audio: SDL/0092 FlushRingSilence -- NO-OP / FAIL "
+                         "(dma_buffer=%p dma_halfdma=%d bpf=%d; returning 0 = "
+                         "DMA not fully zeroable -- partial-coverage fail)",
+                         (void *)isr_dma_buffer, isr_dma_halfdma, bpf);
+        return 0;
+    }
+    struct SDL_PrivateAudioData *hidden = device->hidden;
+    const Uint8 sil = isr_silence_value;
+
+    /* 1. Zero the whole software ring (the staging buffer the ISR drains from).
+     * No cli: a concurrent ISR RingCopyOut at worst pulls one mixed
+     * stale/silence chunk this IRQ; the cursor collapse below makes the ISR
+     * underrun to silence on every IRQ after. */
+    SDL_memset(isr_ring_buffer, sil, isr_ring_size);
+
+    /* 2. Zero the entire DMA double-buffer -- the PHYSICAL buffer the SB16
+     * auto-init DMA cycles end-to-end, and the buffer the DAC actually reads on
+     * underrun. Zeroing it directly makes the HW loop silence on its next
+     * sample fetch even if IRQ-5 refill is delayed during the blocking render.
+     * isr_dma_halfdma is half the DMA buffer; full == x2. Guarded non-NULL
+     * above, so this always runs to completion on the nonzero-return path. */
+    SDL_memset(isr_dma_buffer, sil, isr_dma_halfdma * 2);
+
+    /* 3. Collapse the cursors ISR-atomically: align the ISR drain position to
+     * the frozen producer write position so avail == 0 -> the ISR takes its
+     * underrun-silence memset path. Mirror into the hidden producer cursors so
+     * the audio thread's own bookkeeping stays coherent when it resumes. */
+    DOS_DisableInterrupts();
+    isr_ring_read = isr_ring_write;
+    if (hidden) {
+        hidden->ring_read = isr_ring_write;
+        hidden->ring_write = isr_ring_write;
+    }
+    DOS_EnableInterrupts();
+
+    const int ring_frames = isr_ring_size / bpf;
+    const int dma_frames  = (isr_dma_halfdma * 2) / bpf;
+    const int total_frames = ring_frames + dma_frames;
+    doskutsu_dbg_log("audio: SDL/0092 FlushRingSilence -- ring+DMA zeroed "
+                     "(silence_frames_flushed=%d = ring_frames=%d + "
+                     "dma_frames=%d; ring_bytes=%d dma_bytes=%d) "
+                     "pre-cold-render quiesce",
+                     total_frames, ring_frames, dma_frames,
+                     isr_ring_size, isr_dma_halfdma * 2);
+    /* Nonzero == both buffers fully zeroed (== PhysicalCapacityFrames()). */
+    return total_frames;
+}
+
+/* DOSKUTSU patch (SDL/0092): true total physical silence-flush capacity, in
+ * frames. This is the denominator for the flush-instr witness
+ * silence_frames_flushed >= ring_capacity_frames: the FULL physical depth that
+ * can hold stale music between the engine producer and the DAC -- the software
+ * ring (PlayDevice -> ISR staging, isr_ring_size) PLUS the DMA double-buffer
+ * (the buffer the SB16 auto-init DMA physically cycles, isr_dma_halfdma * 2).
+ *
+ * Deliberately NOT SDL_DOSAudioRingCapacityMs()/RingFillFrames (SDL/0088):
+ * those report the SOFTWARE ring ONLY and miss the DMA double-buffer, which
+ * would let the witness FALSE-PASS while the SB16 still loops the music left
+ * in the DMA buffer. Bind the witness denominator to THIS, not to 0088.
+ *
+ * SDL_DOSAudioFlushRingSilence() returns a frame count equal to this on a full
+ * flush (equal by construction); it returns LESS only if a buffer is
+ * unallocated, which is exactly the partial-coverage signal the witness must
+ * catch. (The upstream SDL mixer output_stream FIFO is not counted: during the
+ * blocking cold-render the audio thread is parked, so nothing advances that
+ * FIFO into the ring -- it cannot reach the DAC within the render window.)
+ * 0 when no device is open. */
+int SDL_DOSAudioPhysicalCapacityFrames(void)
+{
+    if (g_dos_audio_device == NULL || isr_ring_buffer == NULL) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    const int bpf = g_dos_ring_bytes_per_frame;
+    if (bpf <= 0) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    return (isr_ring_size + isr_dma_halfdma * 2) / bpf;
+}
+
 /* ========================================================================
  * DOSKUTSU patch (SDL/0090): device-rate setter (productionization of 0087)
  * ========================================================================
-- 
